Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>11  Dates and a calendar</H1>
<HR>
An organiser is mostly about days, so this chapter is about dates: why JavaScript's own dates are awkward,
a small module that makes them easy, and the diary's four ways of looking at time - a week, a day, a month
and a year. It also gives the binder a toolbox down its left-hand side, as Lotus Organizer had. The program is
<CODE>!Org11</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>JavaScript's dates</H2>
JavaScript has a <CODE>Date</CODE> object, and you met it in the first book's Clock. A Date is a moment in
time - a particular millisecond - and it can tell you the year, month, day and hour of that moment:<P>
<PRE>
const d = new Date(2026, 8, 25, 12);   // 25 September 2026, noon
d.getFullYear()    // 2026
d.getMonth()       // 8 - September!
d.getDate()        // 25
d.getDay()         // 5 - Friday (Sunday is 0)
</PRE>
Three things about it catch everyone out.<P>
<DL>
<DT>Months count from 0
<DD><CODE>new Date(2026, 8, 25)</CODE> is in September, not August: January is month 0. Days of the month
count from 1 as usual, and <CODE>getDay</CODE> counts the days of the week from Sunday, 0.<P>
<DT>It's a moment, not a day
<DD>An appointment is on a <I>day</I>, but a Date is an exact moment, which falls on different days in
different places. Store "25 September" as a Date and it can come back as the 24th on a computer set to
another time zone.<P>
<DT>Summer time
<DD>Twice a year the clocks change, and a day is 23 or 25 hours long. Add 24 hours to midnight on the day the
clocks go back and you land at 11 at night on the same day, not the next day.<P>
</DL>
The organiser avoids all three by hardly using Date at all. A day is written as a <I>key</I>, a string like
<CODE>'2026-09-25'</CODE>: year, month (from 1!) and day, always with two digits for the month and day. Keys
have two lovely properties: comparing them as text compares them as dates (<CODE>'2026-09-25' &lt;
'2026-10-01'</CODE> is true), and they go into files, and into Maps, exactly as they are. Only one module,
<CODE>Dates</CODE>, turns keys into Dates, and it always makes them at midday - far enough from midnight that
no clock change can move them to another day.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>The Dates module, tests first</H2>
Date arithmetic is easy to get nearly right, and the mistakes show up months later ("why is my appointment on
the wrong day in March?"). So this module was written <I>tests first</I>: before writing
<CODE>addMonths</CODE>, write down what it should do, including the awkward cases, in <CODE>Check</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
export default function start() {
  // ------------------------------------------------ dates
  check('add days over a month', D.addDays('2026-09-29', 3),
    '2026-10-02');
  check('add a month to 31 January', D.addMonths('2026-01-31', 1),
    '2026-02-28');
  check('leap years', D.addYears('2024-02-29', 1), '2025-02-28');
  check('a Friday', D.weekday('2026-09-25'), 4);
  check('its Monday', D.startOfWeek('2026-09-25'), '2026-09-21');
  check('days between', D.daysBetween('2026-12-25', '2027-01-01'), 7);
  check('a month grid starts on a Monday',
    D.monthGrid(2026, 9)[0][0], '2026-08-31');
  check('reading dates', [D.parseDay('25/9/2026'),
    D.parseDay('2026-09-25'), D.parseDay('31/02/2026')],
  ['2026-09-25', '2026-09-25', null]);
  check('reading times', [D.parseTime('9:30'), D.parseTime('0930'),
    D.parseTime('25:00')], ['09:30', '09:30', null]);
</PRE>
Each line is a question with its answer worked out by hand: three days after 29 September is 2 October; a
month after 31 January is 28 February, because there is no 31 February; a year after 29 February 2024 is 28
February 2025; 25 September 2026 is a Friday (4, because the module counts the week from Monday, 0); its week
starts on Monday the 21st; the Christmas to New Year gap is 7 days; the calendar for September 2026 starts on
Monday 31 August. Run <CODE>Check</CODE> before the module exists and every test fails; write the module
until they all pass; and whenever you change it later, run it again to see that nothing broke.<P>
This <CODE>Check</CODE> is a new one: chapter 10's tests of <CODE>Collection</CODE> aren't in it, because it
tests what this chapter adds - the dates and the diary. <CODE>Collection</CODE> hasn't changed, and the diary
test below uses it, so nothing is lost this time; but in your own programs, keep the old tests when you add
new ones. They are what notices when a change breaks something that used to work.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O11Check" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
<CODE>Check</CODE> itself has one new trick. Two objects with the same fields in a different order are the
same entry, but <CODE>JSON.stringify</CODE> writes them differently. So <CODE>check</CODE> gives
<CODE>stringify</CODE> a second argument, a <I>replacer</I> function it calls for every value it writes:<P>
<PRE>
// the same, whatever order their fields are in
const sorted = (k, v) =&gt; (v &amp;&amp; typeof v === 'object' &amp;&amp;
  !Array.isArray(v) ? Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(v).sort())
  : v);
function check(what, got, want) {
  const ok = JSON.stringify(got, sorted) ===
    JSON.stringify(want, sorted);
  if (!ok) failed++;
  print(`${ok ? 'ok  ' : 'FAIL'} ${what}`);
  if (!ok) print(`     got ${JSON.stringify(got)}`);
}
</PRE>
For an object (not an array), the replacer returns a copy with its fields in alphabetical order:
<CODE>Object.entries</CODE> makes it a list of <CODE>[name, value]</CODE> pairs, <CODE>sort</CODE> sorts
them by name, and <CODE>Object.fromEntries</CODE> turns the pairs back into an object.<P>
Here is the module. Its header lists what it offers:<P>
<PRE>
// Dates - days written as keys: '2026-09-25'. Keys sort and compare
// as text ('2026-09-25' &lt; '2026-10-01'), make good names for things
// in a Map, and go into files as they are. JavaScript's Date is used
// only inside this module, and always at midday, so that changes to
// and from summer time never move a day.
//
//   today()                       '2026-09-25'
//   keyOf(date) / dateOf(key)     a Date to a key and back
//   addDays(key, n), addMonths(key, n), addYears(key, n)
//   daysBetween(a, b)             whole days from a to b
//   weekday(key)                  0 Monday ... 6 Sunday
//   startOfWeek(key)              the Monday of its week
//   monthGrid(year, month)        6 weeks x 7 keys (Mondays first)
//   daysInMonth(year, month)      (months are 1-12 here)
//   parseDay('25/12/2026')        a key, or null ('2026-12-25' too)
//   parseTime('9:30')             '09:30', or null
//   format(key, '%W3 %ZDY %M3')   the desktop's formatTime codes
</PRE>
Converting between keys and Dates is where the month is adjusted by one (and <CODE>addMonths</CODE>, below,
which does its sums with Date's months):<P>
<PRE>
const two = (n) =&gt; String(n).padStart(2, '0');

export function keyOf(d) {
  return `${d.getFullYear()}-${two(d.getMonth() + 1)}-` +
    two(d.getDate());
}
export function dateOf(key) {
  const [y, m, d] = key.split('-').map(Number);
  return new Date(y, m - 1, d, 12);            // midday
}
export const today = () =&gt; keyOf(new Date());
</PRE>
<CODE>two</CODE> writes a number with two digits: <CODE>padStart</CODE> (the first book's chapter 5) pads
text at the start with spaces, or with its second argument if there is one - <CODE>String(9).padStart(2, '0')</CODE> is
<CODE>'09'</CODE>. <CODE>dateOf</CODE> uses array destructuring (chapter 3) on <CODE>key.split('-').map(Number)</CODE>, which
turns <CODE>'2026-09-25'</CODE> into <CODE>[2026, 9, 25]</CODE>, and makes the Date at 12 o'clock.<P>
Adding days lets Date do the hard part:<P>
<PRE>
export function addDays(key, n) {
  const d = dateOf(key);
  d.setDate(d.getDate() + n);
  return keyOf(d);
}
export function addMonths(key, n) {
  const [y, m, d] = key.split('-').map(Number);
  const first = new Date(y, m - 1 + n, 1, 12);
  // 31 January + 1 month is 28 (or 29) February, not 3 March
  const day = Math.min(d, daysInMonth(first.getFullYear(),
    first.getMonth() + 1));
  first.setDate(day);
  return keyOf(first);
}
export const addYears = (key, n) =&gt; addMonths(key, 12 * n);

export function daysBetween(a, b) {
  return Math.round((dateOf(b) - dateOf(a)) / 86400000);
}
export const weekday = (key) =&gt; (dateOf(key).getDay() + 6) % 7;
export const startOfWeek = (key) =&gt; addDays(key, -weekday(key));
export function daysInMonth(year, month) {
  return new Date(year, month, 0).getDate();   // day 0 of the next
}
</PRE>
<CODE>setDate</CODE> accepts days beyond the end of the month and carries them over: setting 29 September's
day to 32 gives 2 October. <CODE>addMonths</CODE> can't simply do the same with months, because 31 January plus
one month would carry over to 3 March; so it finds the first of the new month, then chooses the day, no later
than the month's last. <CODE>daysInMonth</CODE> uses the same carrying the other way: day 0 of a month is the
last day of the month before, so <CODE>new Date(year, month, 0)</CODE> - with <CODE>month</CODE> counted from
1, which Date takes as the <I>next</I> month - is the last day of the month we want.<P>
Subtracting two Dates gives the milliseconds between them, and a day is 86,400,000 milliseconds;
<CODE>Math.round</CODE> absorbs the extra or missing hour of a clock change. <CODE>weekday</CODE> turns Date's
Sunday-first count into a Monday-first one - <CODE>(day + 6) % 7</CODE> - because in Britain, as in the
organiser, the week starts on Monday.<P>
A calendar page needs every day it shows, in weeks, including the ends of the months before and after:<P>
<PRE>
/** The weeks of a month for a calendar: 6 rows of 7 keys. */
export function monthGrid(year, month) {
  let day = startOfWeek(`${year}-${two(month)}-01`);
  const weeks = [];
  for (let w = 0; w &lt; 6; w++) {
    const week = [];
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; 7; i++) {
      week.push(day);
      day = addDays(day, 1);
    }
    weeks.push(week);
  }
  return weeks;
}
</PRE>
Six weeks is always enough: a month of 31 days that starts on a Sunday spans six rows.<P>
Reading dates and times typed into the dialogue boxes is done here too, so every part of the organiser
accepts the same forms:<P>
<PRE>
/** '25/12/2026' or '2026-12-25' as a key; null if it isn't a date. */
export function parseDay(text) {
  const s = String(text).trim();
  let m = /^(\d{1,2})\/(\d{1,2})\/(\d{4})$/.exec(s);
  const [y, mo, d] = m ? [+m[3], +m[2], +m[1]]
    : (m = /^(\d{4})-(\d{1,2})-(\d{1,2})$/.exec(s))
      ? [+m[1], +m[2], +m[3]] : [];
  if (!y || mo &lt; 1 || mo &gt; 12 || d &lt; 1) return null;
  if (d &gt; daysInMonth(y, mo)) return null;     // 31/02/2026 ...
  return `${y}-${two(mo)}-${two(d)}`;
}
/** '9:30', '09.30' or '0930' as '09:30'; null if it isn't a time. */
export function parseTime(text) {
  const m = /^(\d{1,2})[:.]?(\d\d)$/.exec(String(text).trim());
  if (!m || +m[1] &gt; 23 || +m[2] &gt; 59) return null;
  return `${two(+m[1])}:${m[2]}`;
}

export const format = (key, fmt) =&gt; formatTime(fmt, dateOf(key));
</PRE>
<CODE>parseDay</CODE> tries <CODE>25/12/2026</CODE> first and, if that fails, <CODE>2026-12-25</CODE>. The
second try is inside the condition, as <CODE>(m = /.../.exec(s))</CODE>: as in chapter 3, an assignment is
also an expression, whose value is what was assigned, so the condition is true if the second pattern
matched. Either way it ends
with the year, month and day, checks them - there is no 31 February - and returns a key, or
<CODE>null</CODE>.<P>
<CODE>format</CODE> hands the desktop's <CODE>formatTime</CODE> (appendix C) a Date made from the key, so
<CODE>D.format(key, '%WE %ZDY %MO')</CODE> is <I>Friday 25 September</I>.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>The diary</H2>
An appointment is an entry with a date (a key), a time (<CODE>'09:30'</CODE>, or <CODE>''</CODE> for all
day), an end time and what it is. The diary gets a class of its own, <CODE>Diary</CODE>, which extends
<CODE>Collection</CODE> with one method, to find the appointments in a range of days, and the
<CODE>Document</CODE> now makes its <CODE>diary</CODE> a <CODE>Diary</CODE> instead of a plain
<CODE>Collection</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
// ------------------------------------------------ the diary
// An appointment: { date, time ('09:30' or ''), end, text }.

export class Diary extends Collection {
  /** [{ appt, date }] for the days from..to, in time order. */
  occurrences(from, to) {
    const out = [];
    for (const a of this.all()) {
      for (const date of datesOf(a, from, to)) {
        out.push({ appt: a, date });
      }
    }
    return out.sort((x, y) =&gt; (x.date + (x.appt.time || ''))
      .localeCompare(y.date + (y.appt.time || '')));
  }
}
/** The days an appointment falls on between from and to. */
function datesOf(a, from, to) {
  return a.date &gt;= from &amp;&amp; a.date &lt;= to ? [a.date] : [];
}
</PRE>
It asks <CODE>datesOf</CODE> which days in the range each appointment falls on, and sorts the lot by date and
time together - both are strings that sort correctly, so joining them sorts by date first, then time. For
now an appointment falls on just its own date; in the next chapter appointments can repeat, and only
<CODE>datesOf</CODE> will change.<P>
The Diary section replaces the first blank in the list of sections:<P>
<PRE>
// DiaryPage - the diary: appointments, shown a week across the two
// pages (or a day, a month or the year: the view buttons).
import { Section } from './Paper';
import * as V from './DiaryViews';
import * as D from './Toolkit/Dates';

export class DiaryPage extends Section {
  constructor(org) {
    super(org, { id: 'diary', name: 'Diary', colour: '#6fa8dc',
      views: ['Week', 'Day', 'Month', 'Year'],
      newHelp: 'add an appointment' });
    this.day = D.today();                  // the day we're looking at
    this.days = [];                        // where each day is drawn
  }

  draw(g, L, R) {
    this.days = [];
    V[this.view.toLowerCase()](this, g, L, R);
  }
  /** Remember where a day is drawn (for clicks and drops). */
  dayBox(key, x, y, w, h) { this.days.push({ key, x, y, w, h }); }
  dayAt(x, y) {
    return this.days.find((d) =&gt; x &gt;= d.x &amp;&amp; x &lt; d.x + d.w &amp;&amp;
      y &gt;= d.y &amp;&amp; y &lt; d.y + d.h)?.key ?? null;
  }

  /** Everything to show on a day: its appointments, as
   * { kind, text, ref, time }. */
  itemsOn(key) {
    const doc = this.doc;
    return [
      ...doc.diary.occurrences(key, key).map(({ appt }) =&gt; ({
        kind: 'appt', time: appt.time, text: appt.text,
        ref: { id: appt.id, date: key } })),
    ];
  }
</PRE>
Its <CODE>views</CODE> are <CODE>Week</CODE>, <CODE>Day</CODE>, <CODE>Month</CODE> and <CODE>Year</CODE>;
<CODE>this.day</CODE> is the day being looked at, and every view shows the page that contains it. While
drawing, a view records each day's rectangle with <CODE>dayBox</CODE>, as it records entries with
<CODE>box</CODE>, so <CODE>dayAt</CODE> can say which day is at a point. <CODE>itemsOn</CODE> collects what
to show on a day - for now its appointments; later, to-dos, anniversaries and planner bars will join them -
as small objects with a <CODE>kind</CODE>, a time, a text and a ref.<P>
<CODE>draw</CODE> is one line: <CODE>V[this.view.toLowerCase()](this, g, L, R)</CODE>. At the top of the
file, <CODE>import * as V from './DiaryViews'</CODE> imports <I>everything</I> the module
<CODE>DiaryViews</CODE> exports, as the properties of one object, <CODE>V</CODE>. Its four drawing functions
are <CODE>V.week</CODE>, <CODE>V.day</CODE>, <CODE>V.month</CODE> and <CODE>V.year</CODE>, so
<CODE>V['week']</CODE> is the week function: the view's name chooses the function.
(<CODE>import * as D from './Toolkit/Dates'</CODE>, at the top of every module that uses dates, works the same
way, and makes calls such as <CODE>D.addDays</CODE> easy to spot.)<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Four ways to see the time</H2>
All four views are in <CODE>DiaryViews</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
// DiaryViews - drawing the diary's four views on the binder's pages.
// Each is a function (diary, g, left, right) that draws, and records
// where the days (diary.dayBox) and the entries (diary.box) are.
//   week   Monday to Wednesday on the left, Thursday to Sunday right
//   day    the day on the left, the next day on the right, by hour
//   month  this month on the left, the next one on the right
//   year   January to June on the left, July to December right
import { heading, text, INK, FAINT, RULE } from './Paper';
import * as D from './Toolkit/Dates';

// an entry's line in a day's box; room left for the page corners
const LINE = 16, FOOT = 24;

</PRE>
The week view is the diary's everyday view, as it was in Lotus Organizer: Monday to Wednesday on the left
page, Thursday to Sunday on the right, each day a box with its appointments in time order.<P>
<PRE>
/** One day's box in the week view: its date, then its entries. */
function dayCell(diary, g, key, x, y, w, h) {
  const today = key === D.today(), chosen = key === diary.day;
  g.fillStyle = chosen ? '#eef4fb' : '#ffffff';
  g.fillRect(x, y, w, h);
  g.strokeStyle = RULE;
  g.strokeRect(x + 0.5, y + 0.5, w - 1, h - 1);
  text(g, D.format(key, '%WE %ZDY %MO'), x + 6, y + 11, w - 12,
    `bold 12px Homerton`, today ? '#c00000' : INK);
  diary.dayBox(key, x, y, w, h);
  let ly = y + 22;
  for (const it of diary.itemsOn(key)) {
    if (ly + LINE &gt; y + h) {                    // no more room
      text(g, '...', x + w - 20, y + h - 8, 16);
      break;
    }
    item(diary, g, it, x + 4, ly, w - 8);
    ly += LINE;
  }
}
/** One entry: its time and what it is. */
function item(diary, g, it, x, y, w) {
  const mid = y + LINE / 2;
  text(g, it.time || '', x, mid, 40, 'bold 12px Homerton');
  text(g, it.text, x + 42, mid, w - 42, '12px Homerton');
  diary.box(x, y, w, LINE, it.ref);
}

export function week(diary, g, L, R) {
  const mon = D.startOfWeek(diary.day);
  const sun = D.addDays(mon, 6);
  heading(g, L, 'Diary', diary.colour, D.format(mon, '%MO %CE%YR'));
  heading(g, R, `Week of ${D.format(mon, '%ZDY %M3')}`, diary.colour,
    `to ${D.format(sun, '%ZDY %M3')}`);
  const top = L.y + 44, x0 = 10;
  const hL = Math.floor((L.h - 54 - FOOT) / 3);    // 3 days left
  const hR = Math.floor((R.h - 54 - FOOT) / 4);    // 4 on the right
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    dayCell(diary, g, D.addDays(mon, i), L.x + x0, top + i * hL,
      L.w - 2 * x0, hL - 4);
  }
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {
    dayCell(diary, g, D.addDays(mon, 3 + i), R.x + x0, top + i * hR,
      R.w - 2 * x0 - 16, hR - 4);
  }
}
</PRE>
<CODE>dayCell</CODE> draws one day: its date, red if it's today, shaded if it's the chosen day, then as many
entries as fit, with <CODE>...</CODE> if some don't. <CODE>week</CODE> finds the Monday of the chosen day's
week and draws seven days from it. The days share the page's height less <CODE>FOOT</CODE>, a strip left
empty at the bottom so that no day's box covers the turned-up page corners.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O11Week" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
The month view shows this month on the left and the next on the right, as calendars:<P>
<PRE>
/** A month's calendar in a page: 6 weeks of 7 days. */
function monthPage(diary, g, p, first) {
  const [y, m] = first.split('-').map(Number);
  heading(g, p, D.format(first, '%MO'), diary.colour, String(y));
  const top = p.y + 64, cw = (p.w - 20) / 7;
  const ch = (p.h - 80 - FOOT) / 6;
  ['Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri', 'Sat', 'Sun'].forEach((n, i) =&gt;
    text(g, n, p.x + 12 + i * cw, top - 12, cw,
      'bold 11px Homerton'));
  D.monthGrid(y, m).forEach((wk, r) =&gt; wk.forEach((key, c) =&gt; {
    const x = p.x + 10 + c * cw, yy = top + r * ch;
    const inMonth = +key.slice(5, 7) === m;
    g.strokeStyle = RULE;
    g.strokeRect(x + 0.5, yy + 0.5, cw - 1, ch - 1);
    text(g, String(+key.slice(8)), x + 4, yy + 9, cw - 6,
      key === D.today() ? 'bold 12px Homerton' : '11px Homerton',
      inMonth ? (key === D.today() ? '#c00000' : INK) : FAINT);
    if (!inMonth) return;
    diary.dayBox(key, x, yy, cw, ch);
    // as many entries as there's room for, each cut short to fit
    const items = diary.itemsOn(key);
    const room = Math.floor((ch - 20) / 11);          // lines of 11
    items.slice(0, room).forEach((it, k) =&gt; {
      const ty = yy + 24 + k * 11;
      text(g, it.text, x + 8, ty, cw - 11, '9px Homerton',
        it.kind === 'appt' ? INK : FAINT);
    });
    if (items.length &gt; room) {
      text(g, `+${items.length - room}`, x + cw - 16, yy + 9, 14,
        '9px Homerton', FAINT);
    }
  }));
}
export function month(diary, g, L, R) {
  const first = `${diary.day.slice(0, 7)}-01`;
  monthPage(diary, g, L, first);
  monthPage(diary, g, R, D.addMonths(first, 1));
}
</PRE>
The height of a row, <CODE>ch</CODE>, leaves room for the corners with <CODE>FOOT</CODE> in the same way.
<CODE>monthGrid</CODE> gives the days; days that belong to the months before and after are shown faintly,
aren't recorded with <CODE>dayBox</CODE>, and show no entries. Each day shows as many of its entries as its
box has room for, in a small size, and <CODE>+2</CODE> if two more didn't fit.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O11Month" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
The year view is six small months on each page, with days that have entries in bold blue, so you can see at a
glance where the busy weeks are. Clicking a day in the month or year view goes to that day's week. The day view
shows two days, by the hour from 8:00 to 20:00, with anything without a time at the top.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O11Year" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Getting about</H2>
The page corners, and Page Up and Page Down, turn the diary's pages. How far depends on the view:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ turning pages
  turn(dir) {
    const d = this.day;
    this.day = this.view === 'Week' ? D.addDays(d, 7 * dir)
      : this.view === 'Day' ? D.addDays(d, 2 * dir)
      : this.view === 'Month' ? D.addMonths(d, 2 * dir)
      : D.addYears(d, dir);
  }
  today() { this.day = D.today(); }
  key(ev) {                         // the arrow keys move a day/week
    const step = { 0x18C: -1, 0x18D: 1,             // left, right
      0x18F: -7, 0x18E: 7 }[ev.code];               // up, down
    if (!step || this.view !== 'Week') return false;
    this.day = D.addDays(this.day, step);
    this.org.refresh();
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>turn</CODE> is a chain of conditional operators, one view per line: a week at a time in the week view,
two days in the day view (the two pages), two months in the month view and a year in the year view.<P>
In the week view the arrow keys move the chosen day: left and right a day, up and down a week. The step comes
from an object used as a table: <CODE>{ 0x18C: -1, 0x18D: 1, 0x18F: -7, 0x18E: 7 }[ev.code]</CODE> looks up
the key's code in it, giving the number of days, or <CODE>undefined</CODE> for any other key. Home goes back to
today: the window's <CODE>key</CODE> method calls <CODE>section.today()</CODE> for it (the Address section's
<CODE>today</CODE> does nothing), and the window's menu gains <I>View</I>, listing the section's views, and
<I>Today</I>.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>A toolbox</H2>
The toolbox down the left of the window is chapter 5's <CODE>Toolbar</CODE>, with the option we didn't use
then: <CODE>side: 'left'</CODE> makes a column of buttons, like Draw's toolbox, instead of a row. The binder
moves right to make room: <CODE>SIDE</CODE>, which was 0, is now the toolbox's width:<P>
<PRE>
const W = 900, H = 540, SIDE = 64;         // the toolbox's width
</PRE>
<PRE>
    this.binder = new Binder({ x: SIDE, y: 0, w: W - SIDE, h: H,
      colour: this.app.prefs.colour,
      tabs: this.sections.map((s) =&gt; ({ name: s.name,
        colour: s.colour })) });
    this.bar = new Toolbar(w, {
      side: 'left', width: SIDE - 8,
      menu: (ev) =&gt; this.openMenu(ev), buttons: tools.buttons(this),
    });
</PRE>
The toolbox and the dates are the first toolkit modules the organiser adds since chapter 10, so
<CODE>!Org11</CODE>'s <CODE>Toolkit.list</CODE> gains two lines, <CODE>Toolbar</CODE> and
<CODE>Dates</CODE>. What the buttons are and do is in a module of its own, <CODE>Tools</CODE>, which will grow
in the next few chapters:<P>
<PRE>
// Tools - the toolbox down the left of the binder: Today (it shows
// today's date: click it to go to today) and the view buttons, which
// change with the section (the Diary's Week, Day, Month and Year).
import * as D from './Toolkit/Dates';

/** Today's date for the toolbox: '25 Sep'. */
export const TODAY = () =&gt; D.format(D.today(), '%ZDY %M3');

const VIEWS = 4;                          // room for 4 view buttons
export function buttons(org) {
  const view = (i) =&gt; ({ name: 'view' + i, text: '', h: 26,
    action: () =&gt; { const s = org.section;
      s.setView(s.views[i]); org.refresh(); } });
  return [
    { name: 'today', text: TODAY(), h: 30,
      action: () =&gt; { org.section.today(); org.refresh(); },
      help: 'Today\'s date. Click SELECT to go to today.' },
    { gap: true },
    ...Array.from({ length: VIEWS }, (_, i) =&gt; view(i)),
  ];
}
/** The toolbox follows the section: its view buttons. */
export function updateToolbox(org) {
  const s = org.section, bar = org.bar;
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; VIEWS; i++) {
    bar.hide('view' + i, i &gt;= s.views.length);
    if (i &lt; s.views.length) {
      bar.setText('view' + i, s.views[i]);
      const on = s.views[i] === s.view;
      bar.icon('view' + i).setState({ selected: on });
    }
  }
  bar.setText('today', TODAY());
}
</PRE>
The first button shows today's date and goes to it. The rest are for the views - but each section has
different views (the Diary has four, the Address section none), so there are four spare buttons, which
<CODE>updateToolbox</CODE> relabels, hides and selects to match the section. <CODE>Array.from({ length: VIEWS },
(_, i) =&gt; view(i))</CODE> makes an array of four buttons: <CODE>Array.from</CODE> (chapter 8), given an
object with a <CODE>length</CODE>, makes an array of that length, calling the function for each item with its
index (the first argument, the item, is
<CODE>undefined</CODE> here, so it's called <CODE>_</CODE>, a name that says "not used"). The spread
<CODE>...</CODE> puts the four into the list of buttons.<P>
The window calls <CODE>updateToolbox</CODE> from <CODE>refresh</CODE>, which it calls whenever the section
or its view changes:<P>
<PRE>
  /** The toolbox follows the section; then redraw. */
  refresh() {
    tools.updateToolbox(this);
    this.redraw();
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>import * as tools from './Tools'</CODE> brings in the module as one object, so the calls read
<CODE>tools.updateToolbox(this)</CODE>.<P>

